From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gustavo Almeida Barrios <gabarrios2006@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 01:37:35 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] Recover a lost MLow packet from the next PT 121 packet's
 redundant copy

A PT 121 (mlow-red) packet repeats earlier frames in its SplitRed blocks,
but only its main frame was decoded: a lost packet was concealed (invented
from the pitch of the last frame) even when the packet after it carried
the lost frame itself.

What a block's time code means is not documented, so no block is trusted
by its position: a position is used only after the stream proved it, with
a block equal byte for byte to the main frame of the packet that many
sequence numbers back. Then, when the packet after a loss arrives, the
repeated frame is decoded in place of the lost one, as a received frame
(it advances the decoder state and reaches the timed NetEq sink); older
losses the blocks do not cover are still concealed. A sender whose
redundancy is a different encoding never proves a position and keeps the
concealment it had. A block equal to several of the remembered main frames
(identical silence or comfort-noise frames at the start of a call), or
seen before three main frames are known, proves nothing: a wrong distance
would replay an older frame on every later recovery. The audio receive
summary now counts concealed and recovered frames, and the first proven
position is logged once.

Tested: a PT 121 stream that loses a packet gives the lost frame back
sample for sample as a lossless receiver decodes it, with no concealment
and the same decoder state afterwards; before the redundancy is proven the
loss is concealed as before; re-encoded redundancy is never trusted;
identical leading main frames prove no distance; full suite passes.
